# Encryption and keys

## Field-level encryption (prisma-field-encryption)

- Encrypted fields are annotated in app/prisma/schema.prisma comments, e.g.:
  - User.email, User.password
  - AccountRegister.name, plaidId, plaidAccessToken, walletAddress
  - RegisterEntry.description, plaidId
  - Reoccurrence.description
- Some fields have deterministic hash columns for lookup (e.g., plaidAccessTokenHash, plaidIdHash, emailHash) with explicit algorithms in comments
- Generated helpers under app/prisma/reencrypt/ support migration/re-encryption across keys

## RSA and JWT

- Rsa model stores keypairs (id, isDefault); server/services/RsaService.ts manages RSA operations
- JwtService.ts and HashService.ts provide token and hashing utilities (with tests)

## Plaid token handling

- server/lib/plaidAccessTokenCrypto.ts — crypto logic for Plaid tokens
- AccountRegister stores Plaid access tokens encrypted with separate hash columns for lookups
- Plaid sync activity is logged in PlaidSyncLog (counts, durations, errors)

## Key rotation (staging CronJob)

- .deploy/key-rotation-cronjob.template.yaml uses a special microservice image target (runner-keyrotate) to:
  - Patch K8S secret ${SECRET_DB} containing only DB encryption key materials (DB_ENCRYPTION_KEY and any DB_DECRYPTION_KEY_n)
  - Roll deployments (main and microservice)
  - Call microservice routes/migrate.post.ts to apply re-encryption/database migrations as needed
- RBAC for rotating secrets and patching deployments is in .deploy/key-rotation-rbac.template.yaml

## Post-restore plan (local dev)

- docs/post-restore-encryption-plan.md describes the approach:
  - Preferred: run generated prisma/reencrypt migrate() with both source and local keys to re-encrypt
  - Fallback (when source key is unavailable): overwrite sensitive columns with placeholders, null Plaid columns, and use friendly sample names for users/registers/entries/reoccurrences
  - Wire-up via scripts/dump-and-restore.sh and scripts/reset-encrypted-users-after-restore.ts

## Secrets layout (K8S)

- SECRET_DB — only DB field-encryption secrets (not DATABASE_URL)
- SECRET_DATABASE_MAIN / SECRET_DATABASE_MICRO — contain DATABASE_URL for each service
- SECRET_APP — app secrets (POSTMARK, PLAID, INTERNAL_API_TOKEN, etc.)
- SECRET_JWT — DINEROS_JWT_SECRET

Follow deployment.mdc for how these are injected. Do not log or commit any key material.
